This is a dirty patch which restores the gallium-xa feature, which was removed
from upstream Mesa as of 25.2, but is required by the Xorg driver
xf86-video-vmware (otherwise the build of the driver silently disables 3D
acceleration support). This driver is needed when running as a guest under
either VMware or VirtualBox (both use the VMware SVGA graphics adapter).

Upstream recommends switching to modesetting, which does *work*, but is
imperfect. Namely, the auto-resize feature of VMware doesn't work with the
modesetting driver. Presumably a fix is needed in open-vm-tools for this. Until
such time that a fix makes its way into open-vm-tools, the next best solution
we could come up with for MassOS is to simply restore the gallium-xa feature,
acknowledging that it is unmaintained and may be buggy, but the MassOS
developers have decided that it's the best stop-gap in this case. It will be
removed once open-vm-tools gets fixed to work with the modesetting driver.

See the following upstream issue for more information:

  https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/xorg/driver/xf86-video-vmware/-/issues/12

This patch makes minor modifications to the code of gallium-xa, so as to allow
the component to build under Mesa 25.3 and higher, which has introduced some
API changes (such as certain macros being renamed).

With this patch, the gallium-xa feature is still treated as deprecated and
disabled by default. To enable it, you will need to pass the build parameter
'-Dgallium-xa=enabled'.

